WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:00.738
Hardening.

2
00:00:00.738 --> 00:00:04.473
CyberArk's hardening section
currently covers IIS only:

3
00:00:04.473 --> 00:00:07.493
a rewrite rule against
host-header injection;

4
00:00:07.493 --> 00:00:13.057
web configuration settings with debugging
off, cookies marked HTTP-only and secure,

5
00:00:13.057 --> 00:00:17.110
request filtering to GET and
POST, strict transport security,

6
00:00:17.110 --> 00:00:22.276
frame and content security policy headers;
and the X-Powered-By header removed.

7
00:00:22.276 --> 00:00:23.425
Apply all of it.

8
00:00:23.425 --> 00:00:26.042
Every platform server
ships with a self-signed

9
00:00:26.042 --> 00:00:29.263
certificate called the Venafi
Operational Certificate.

10
00:00:29.263 --> 00:00:33.837
Replace it on every server with one
issued by your CA before production.

11
00:00:33.837 --> 00:00:39.290
The database needs two accounts: a DBO
account used only to install and upgrade,

12
00:00:39.290 --> 00:00:42.562
and an operational account
the services run as.

13
00:00:42.562 --> 00:00:47.294
CyberArk documents that the operational
account needs Log on as a Service and local

14
00:00:47.294 --> 00:00:49.998
administrator rights on
every platform server,

15
00:00:49.998 --> 00:00:52.837
and group managed service
accounts are supported.

16
00:00:52.837 --> 00:00:56.125
The provisioning drivers need
privilege on the targets too.

17
00:00:56.125 --> 00:00:59.846
For Windows CAPI and IIS,
CyberArk says the account

18
00:00:59.846 --> 00:01:03.213
must be a Windows
administrator on the target.

19
00:01:03.213 --> 00:01:07.349
The program's guidance is to keep those
credentials in CyberArk's Enterprise

20
00:01:07.349 --> 00:01:11.550
Password Vault, which the platform can
query through the Credential Provider.

21
00:01:11.550 --> 00:01:15.325
And the installation answer
files contain the admin password,

22
00:01:15.325 --> 00:01:19.100
the database credentials and the
software key, so encrypt them.

23
00:01:19.100 --> 00:01:24.063
So the platform concentrates privilege:
certificate-manager rights on CAs,

24
00:01:24.063 --> 00:01:28.475
administrator on Windows targets,
and private keys in its database.

25
00:01:28.475 --> 00:01:31.695
The program therefore
treats the Master Admins,

26
00:01:31.695 --> 00:01:35.075
the software key and the
database backups as Tier 0.

27
00:01:35.075 --> 00:01:40.397
